{
  "id": "romanian_romanta_mahala",
  "name": "Romanță and cântec de mahala (Bucharest urban song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Urban song of Bucharest. The romanță, a sentimental strophic love song in Romanian that grew from nineteenth-century salon song and the Russian romance, was sung to piano, violin or small orchestra; the cântec de mahala (suburb song) was its lăutar counterpart in the city's outer neighbourhoods, sung with Ottoman-tinged melisma over violin, accordion, cimbalom and double bass. Both reached gramophone records in the interwar years through singers such as Zavaidoc and Jean Moscopol and were recorded after the war by Electrecord with Ioana Radu, Gică Petrescu and Maria Tănase.",
  "recipe": "classic Eastern European early-1940s romanță and cântec de mahala, functional Song, europop branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. melodic bowed song accompaniment fiddle, accordion, portable no pedal romanian taraf tradition lap played cimbalom, kolstein bass specific bowed song accompaniment double bass, upright piano. hardwood living. large-diaphragm condenser, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, passive program EQ. tape 15ips plate reverb.",
  "recipe_chars": 469,
